WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.228
Take twelve parts off one machine. Same tool, same gauge.

2
00:00:05.316 --> 00:00:08.984
Every reading is different. Nothing is broken.

3
00:00:09.083 --> 00:00:14.191
Spread is not a defect. It is what every real process does.

4
00:00:14.283 --> 00:00:19.295
Twelve numbers is not an answer. You need one number for the centre.

5
00:00:19.383 --> 00:00:22.643
The mean is the balance point of the measurements.

6
00:00:22.733 --> 00:00:25.472
The deviations above the mean add to

7
00:00:25.572 --> 00:00:28.153
plus zero point zero six two five.

8
00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:31.112
Below it, the same amount with the

9
00:00:31.212 --> 00:00:34.510
opposite sign. The sum is exactly zero.

10
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.488
Now spread. Averaging the deviations is

11
00:00:38.588 --> 00:00:41.608
useless, they cancel by construction.

12
00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:47.240
Square each one. Negatives turn positive and the cancelling stops.

13
00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:52.513
Average the squares. That is the variance, in millimetres squared.

14
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:57.180
Take the root and you are back in millimetres. That is sigma.

15
00:00:57.966 --> 00:01:02.258
Twelve parts say nothing about shape. Keep measuring.

16
00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:06.162
Sixty. Three hundred. Three thousand.

17
00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:10.830
Twenty thousand parts, and a shape appears that nobody chose.

18
00:01:10.916 --> 00:01:14.253
Many small independent effects add up. The

19
00:01:14.353 --> 00:01:17.608
bell is a consequence, not an assumption.

20
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.491
You never measure everything. The true mean

21
00:01:21.591 --> 00:01:23.940
and spread belong to the process.

22
00:01:24.033 --> 00:01:26.813
Your twelve parts only estimate them.

23
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:31.168
Four more handfuls of twelve land somewhere else every time.

24
00:01:31.266 --> 00:01:35.846
That is also why the sample spread divides by n minus one.

25
00:01:35.933 --> 00:01:41.425
Every estimate carries uncertainty. Level one puts a number on it.

